  "answers": [
    "**Your average for October 1\u20133, 2026 is either 2,000 or 6,000 steps per day, depending on how you treat the two zero days. I'd report 6,000 as the more accurate figure.**\n\n- **October 1:** 0 steps\n- **October 2:** 0 steps\n- **October 3:** 6,000 steps\n\nThe two zeros are flagged as \"zero may mean no data.\" A zero step count usually means the tracker wasn't worn, not that you walked nothing. Sedentary minutes are also missing for October 3, which fits a device that wasn't worn or didn't sync that day.\n\n- **Counting the zeros:** (0 + 0 + 6,000) \u00f7 3 = **2,000 steps/day**. This treats the no-wear days as days with no activity, so it understates your typical activity.\n- **Excluding the zeros:** only October 3 has real data, so the average is **6,000 steps/day**, based on a single day.\n\nMissing or no-wear days pull the average down when they're counted as zero, and they can make it look like a sedentary stretch when there wasn't one. Excluding them gives a fairer picture, but it rests on fewer days, so one day is a weak basis for a trend. The 6,000 figure shouldn't be read as your normal level.\n\nI can't tell from this data whether the tracker was off or the day was truly inactive. If you want a more reliable average, pull a longer date range so more days with real readings are included."
  ],
